This is some funky mojo.

Looking at the management scripts written by our various admins over the
last decade, the YP master machine (apparently) locks the file
/var/yp/src/passwd.ptmp when someone is changing their password, and
/var/yp/src/ptmp when new accounts are being generated.  Many of our
account generation/manipulation scripts do test-and-set operations on
those two files.

As far as chfn/chsh (or "yppasswd -f" and "yppasswd -s") go, the
yppasswds on our YP clients are all perl scripts, to make sure a new
password isn't a brain-damaged choice before contacting the YP master.
I believe that -f or -s just pass the info along (although I really
don't remember).

Hope this helps.  Or, at the least, hope this doesn't make things worse.


