Chang, Yu Y wrote:
> 
> I'm looking for a good web page that explains the
> OSF multi-thread model.
> I have been reading this newsgroup a while, and haven't
> really seen any posts on OSF thread programming.
> I would like to correspond with someone who has done
> some thread programming on OSF.
> Please email me if you know something about OSF threads.
> 

By "OSF threads" do you mean "DCE threads" (that is
the thread implimentation that ships with DCE) or
"Operating System Threads" (that is Mach threads)?

OSF/1 (various vendors, lets use DEC) supported an
implimentation of DCE, also recomended by the OSF.
The threads model used there is an early POSIX draft
(Draft 4, or 7 depending on your OS). You can find
documentation on that model in either the Nutshell
Pthreads Book or the OSF documentation.

OSF/1 was (is?) based on Mach, and Mach is itself
multi-threaded. You can find documentation on Mach
threads in "Programming Under Mach". I am not aware
of any OSF/1 vendor that supports the Mach interfaces
for user programs.

Other than that, vendors are moving to the POSIX
standard threads model. The philosophy is the same
as the Draft 4 (DCE) threads, but the interfaces
and some semantics are different (ie, join/detach).

Brian.
