tls@panix.com (Thor Lancelot Simon) writes:

>I recall quite a bit of hardware that was supported by "Solaris 2.0->2.3"
>which was *not* supported by SunOS4.1.3 until other SPARC vendors did it
>themselves and Sun was forced to.  Sun downright *coerced* its customers to
>use early Solaris 2 versions, and now they're surprised that Solaris has a bad
>reputation in consequence?

Quite a bit?  The only hardware that didn't run under SunOS 4.1.3
and wasn't supported for some time were the Classic and the LX;
the MP stuff in the SS10 wasn't supported, but many people ran it without
trouble.  And guess what?  Sun actually listened and started supporting
the Classic and LX, the SS4, the SS5 and the SS20 with the exception
of some options that would have required a more substantial engineering
effort.

>Peter -- somewhere around 2.5 or 2.5.1, Solaris actually got usable.  Rumor
>has it that this is because certain of the Solaris developers were forced to
>run it themselves, instead of SunOS. :-)  It might be worth another look.

Even 2.3 (no more NeWS; more or less true to form X11) was usable,
and I think I heard it was during development of 2.3 that many engineering
servers were upgraded to SC2000/SS1000 class machines that didn't
run anything but Solaris, so I guess there was some incentive to get it
right :-)  I wasn't at Sun back then, but it was also during the 2.3
timeframe Sun moved most of its desktop and production servers over to
Solaris 2.3.  And it only makes sense; the more people run software the
better it gets.  Whether this required much force, I don't know.
The latest hardware is worth some sacrifices, I'd imagine.

Of course, I had been running Solaris 2.x for quite some time at the
University from 2.1 on.  We'd even run production on 2.1, but we
didn't go for the "big push" ourslves until 2.3.  New hardware did
get installed w/ Solaris almost without exception even before that time.

Jumpstart was the big thing for us then; no post installation tinkering
once you've set it up.  Roll in 50 machines; take the ethernet
addresses and you'd need more time to unpack and connect the machines
than to install them (which boiled down to "boot net - install").

I've upgraded an entire cluster of machines from SunOS 4.x to
Solaris 2.x from behind my desk.

Casper
