Torsten Hehl wrote:
> 
> We have a a system of 11 ALPHAS running DUNIX and 5 boxes with ULTRIX
> installed in our institute. We run NIS (which is not very save, I know)
> and therefore only base security level is used.
> We automount a lot of discs over the system, and the two SysAds trust
> each other (at least I do). So far, so good.

> Our institute just bought a number of PCs, where we intend to install
> Linux. The users of the PCs want to become root to do local
> administration (as they do at home). On the other side they intend to
> share the automounted disks and the NIS-system to see their usual home
> directory on the Alpha server as well as their  data distributed all
> over the system. Of course I exported all file systems with root mapped
> to nobody, but this does not prevent the local root on the Linux from
> doing a su to any account, which gives them r/w access to all imported
> disks.

We have almost the same network structure, except that there are fewer
alphas
and more PCs :-(. We found this to be very effective in terms of price
to value 
ratio, since you get enough personal workstations and alpha computing
power for 
everyone. Allowing the PCs to access shared data requires NFS, though.
This definitely *is* a security hole. 
 
> The only solution I see is to find a trusted Linux administrator, or is
> their any other solution? Even without NIS root can create an account in
> his /etc/passwd faking any user he likes.

AFAIK this is the only way to do it as long as you have pure NFS.
Despite this,
things work quite well in our place. Make sure that people cannot easily
become 
root on their respective machines: Set boot option to HD, secure BIOS
setup using
a password, and, of course, keep your root password secret. As long as
you keep
the /etc/passwd identical, everything should work fine. 

Of course, some evil-minded user could still open his box and disconnect
the CMOS
battery in order to delete the password protection. So, things described
here are
mainly good for protecting people from their own curiosity. If you're
serious
about security, consider using Kerberos or the like (we are also
thinking about it).

Good Luck
Martin

