X-Deja-AN: 202689006
organization: Asia Technology Inc
keywords: threads sockets
newsgroups: comp.programming.threads,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.solaris,comp.unix.programmer,comp.os.vms


I am assessing the possibility of replacing a network communications
subsystem with a TCP/IP implementation using pthreads. It is the
first pthreaded application I have attempted , but I have a lot
of experience of this type of application on various UNIX systems
using sockets and fork(ing).

At the moment the sub-system runs as a server on VMS and a client
on MS-Windows using UDP on one PORT. Built into the comms layer is
an in-house reliable connection mechanism between the VMS server
MS-Windows clients.

The current sub-system works for about 550 connections but it is
starting to become flaky as more clients are added. 

The development (of the server software) will be done on Solaris
x86 V 2.5.0 and Digital UNIX 4.0. 

Ideally the server code should be portable to a VMS platform.

Is is there anything that we need to do to the kernels of the
3 systems to allow for 1000 TCP connections?

Is there any software on the Net which does this task or 
something similar.

If not then at the moment I envisage having :

   One thread accepting connections and creating one new thread
   per connection. (Accepter)

   One thread read-blocking on the each socket connection. Each 
   thread will write to an internal IPC that is connected to the
   main application.(Reader)

   One thread will read from an internal IPC to receive processed
   messages from the main application. (Dispatcher)

   One thread per-connection which will block waiting for the 
   Dispatcher to send it a message with it will send out onto
   the network. (Writer)

Is there a simple method by which the Dispatcher can multiplex
to the correct Writer (assuming that any info needed to do this
can be prefixed to the outgoing message by the main application)
Or will I be forced to implement some sort of structure/lookup table
containing a mutex lock for each Writer?

Is there a better way to write this sort of code?


