In article <1996Dec6.091744@kuhub.cc.ukans.edu>, paul@kuhub.cc.ukans.edu
(Craig Paul) wrote:

>How does one get tcpdump into promisuous mode and also sniffing icmp
>and other IP packets not destined for it but on the same wire?
>
>If involves more than
>
>MAKEDEV pfilt
>pfconfig ln0 promisc
>ifconfig -i ln0 promisc (or something like that)
>tcpdump ....

First you need to have the PACKETFILTER configured into your kernel

    /usr/sys/conf/<HOSTNAME>
    ...
    options PACKETFILTER
    ...

    doconfig -c <HOSTNAME>
    
    copy new kernel to /vmunix (or some other name if you prefer)
    reboot the system specifying the kernel with the PACKETFILTER code

With a kernel booted that has the PACKETFILTER, I use the following to
start up tcpdumps

    pfconfig +p +c tu0
    tcpdump -w /tmp/tcpdump.raw host xxx and yyy

Personally, I tend to limit my filtering to just host selection and capture
everything into a raw file.  I then do my analysis later.  And to date I
have only been concerned with NFS and related protocols.

