Take a look at name_cache.c for an alternative approach in our
book, "Programming with Threads".  The examples may be downloaded
from:

http://www.Sun.COM:80/smi/ssoftpress/threads/index.html

Spawning 1000 threads may make sense if you have a really big MP
and the duty cycle (%active time) of each thread is really high.
For most systems where clients are idle most of the time (due to 
think time, etc), dispatching each actual request to a thread
will result in less resource consumption, better cache behavior, 
etc.

For example, supppose in the limiting case only one client request
was active at a time.  Using separate threads would force each 
thread stack to be brought into the cache (or worse, into memory),
while simply using a LIFO queue of service threads would produce
much better performance.

In general, controlling the amount of concurrency using a workpile
style technique results in more easily tuned applications, and ones
that behave more reasonably under unreasonable loads.

- Bart

